+
 ## Choosing one
 
 The system's entry in the config file names each app, and contains the
@@ -113,3 +116,45 @@ Copy `none.sh` and fill in the functions. Two rules.
   `jd new` reads stdout.
 - Say what is wrong on stderr, with `_jd_nav_err`, and return 1. The
   user must not have to read an error from the app itself.
+
+## JDex stores
+
+jd reads and writes a JDex that is a folder of Markdown notes itself. A
+JDex in any other app needs a store: `jdex/<app>.sh`. jd loads it when
+the system's `jdex.app` names it, and uses it for every command that
+reads or writes the JDex: `jdex 11.11`, `jd new id`, `jd new wp`, and an
+ID that jd creates from the JDex. `jdex/bear.sh` is the store for Bear.
+
+An app with no file in `jdex/`, such as `obsidian`, is a folder of
+notes. `jdex/none.sh` is never loaded. It lists the functions, and is the
+file to copy.
+
+A store has its own words for two things.
+
+- A **container** holds notes: a category, or the work package area. In
+  Bear it is a tag, such as `10-19 Life admin/11 Me`.
+- A **locator** is one match, which jd hands back to the store to open.
+  In Bear it is `bear:note:<id>` or `bear:tag:<tag>`.
+
+| Function | What it does |
+| --- | --- |
+| `jd_jdex_load` | Read the JDex, once in each shell. Print why not, and return 1, if it cannot be read |
+| `jd_jdex_find` | The same arguments as `_jd_jdex_find` in `lib/jdex.sh`: `area 10-19`, `category 11`, `id 11.11`, `wp W0012`, `search word...`, `search-area 10-19 word...`, `search-category 11 word...`. Print one line for each match: the locator, the display (a path from the top of the JDex), and the name with no extension, separated by a tab |
+| `jd_jdex_open` | Open one match. `$1` the locator, `$2` the display, for the message |
+| `jd_jdex_open_app` | Open the JDex itself: `jdex` with no target |
+| `jd_jdex_titles` | The note titles directly in container `$1`, one per line. With `$2` `wp`, the work package notes in work package area `$1`. jd reads the numbers from them |
+| `jd_jdex_category` | The container for category `$1`. One line for each match |
+| `jd_jdex_wp_area` | The work package area's container. With none yet, the one that writing the first work package note creates |
+| `jd_jdex_where` | Describe note `$1` in container `$2`, for the line `jd new` prints |
+| `jd_jdex_write` | Create note `$1` in container `$2`. Its text is on stdin, and may be empty. Print its link on stdout. Print nothing if it failed |
+
+A store may set these when it is loaded.
+
+| Variable | Value |
+| --- | --- |
+| `_JD_JDEX_LABEL` | its name in messages, as in `the JDex in Bear` |
+| `_JD_JDEX_UNIT` | what a container is called in messages: `tag` |
+| `_JD_JDEX_EXT` | the extension of the templates jd reads for it: `ID template.<ext>` in a `.03`, `Work package template.<ext>` in `W0003`. Default `md` |
+
+It can read `$_JD_JDEX_PATH`, `$_JD_JDEX_VAULT` and `$_JD_JDEX_SYS`, from
+the config, and the `_JD_NEW_*` variables while `jd new` runs.
diff --git a/lib/adapters/jdex/bear.sh b/lib/adapters/jdex/bear.sh
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9137bd5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/adapters/jdex/bear.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,54 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+# jdex/bear.sh - a JDex in Bear
+#
+# lib/bear.sh reads and writes Bear, through bearcli. This file is the
+# store: the functions that jd calls, each one a call to lib/bear.sh.
+#
+# A container is a tag, with no '#': '10-19 Life admin/11 Me'. A
+# locator is bear:note:<id> or bear:tag:<tag>.
+
+_JD_JDEX_LABEL=Bear
+_JD_JDEX_UNIT=tag
+
+jd_jdex_load() { _jd_bear_load; }
+
+jd_jdex_find() { _jd_bear_find "$@"; }
+
+jd_jdex_open() { _jd_bear_open "$@"; }
+
+jd_jdex_open_app() { _jd_bear_open_app; }
+
+jd_jdex_titles() { _jd_bear_titles "$@"; }
+
+jd_jdex_category() {
+  _jd_bear_find category "$1" | cut -f1 | sed 's/^bear:tag://'
+}
+
+# The work package area tag. With none yet, the tag that the first work
+# package note creates.
+jd_jdex_wp_area() {
+  local jw
+  jw=$(_jd_bear_wp_areas) || return 1
+  [ -n "$jw" ] || jw="$_JD_BEAR_BASE$_JD_NEW_WP_AREA"
+  printf '%s\n' "$jw"
+}
+
+jd_jdex_where() { printf 'Bear: %s, #%s#' "$1" "$2"; }
+
+# The note's text is on stdin. Bear's note starts with the title and
+# the tag, then a blank line and the text.
+jd_jdex_write() {
+  local body id
+  body=$(
+    cat
+    printf x
+  )
+  body=${body%x}
+  id=$(
+    if [ -n "$body" ]; then
+      printf '\n%s' "$body"
+    fi | _jd_bear_create "$1" "$2"
+  )
+  [ -n "$id" ] || return 1
+  printf 'bear://x-callback-url/open-note?id=%s' "$id"
+}
